summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Right elbow pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Carolyn Johnson is a 49-year-old female who presents for follow-up of her chronic problems. She is here today for her right elbow pain. She states that her osteoarthritis has been flaring up a little bit lately. She states that it is mostly in her elbows and it has been bothering her. She states that she has been sitting at the computer, at the desk more in the office so she does not know if it is being stagnant in that position all day has increased flare-ups for that. She wants to talk about that.

She denies any other joint pain. She states that she is right handed. She states that one elbow bothers her more than the other, but primarily she is typing all day versus writing, so it flares up in both. She denies any numbing or tingling in her hands. She states that sometimes when she is typing for long periods of time she feels a little bit of numbing. She tries to shake out her arms a little bit to kind of relieve that sensation. She states that she has had this history of gout. She had an episode of inflammation of her toe 3 months ago. She states that she had some inflammation of her toe 3 months ago. She states that the medication you gave her really controlled it and she has not seen a flare-up since. She states that her psoriasis has been under control too. She states that she has not been a major problem for her. She states that she is doing well with her clobetasol for her scalp. She states that it is doing a lot better.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.
Skin: Reports psoriasis.
Gastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain.
Neurological: Denies numbness or tingling in her hands.
Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Constitutional: In general appearance well-nourished.
Neurological: No focal neurologic deficits.
Musculoskeletal: Right elbow: There is edema and inflammation of the right olecranon. There is some tenderness and an effusion right there. Pain to palpation of the olecranon bursa. Pain with pronation and supination. Pain with flexion and extension of the right arm.

RESULTS

Autoimmune panel: All normal.
ESR: 2 mg/dL.
Uric acid: 6.4 mg/dL.

ASSESSMENT

1. Gout.
2. Osteoarthritis.
3. Psoriasis.

PLAN

For her gout, I will continue her on the colchicine 0.6 mg twice a day as needed for flare-ups of her gout.
For her osteoarthritis, I will order an x-ray of her right elbow. I will also prescribe meloxicam 15 mg once a day for the pain and the swelling. I will see the results of the x-ray and the results of that before we refer her for any type of physical therapy.
From a psoriasis standpoint, I think everything is stable. We will continue her on the steroid cream for her scalp.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>
