Left elbow pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Anna is a 26-year-old female with a past medical history significant for epilepsy, depression, and peptic ulcer disease who presents with left elbow pain.

The patient reports that her left elbow has been bothering her for the past few days. She states that she sits at her desk at work and types, staying in a position with her left elbow bent for an extended period of time. She has difficulty opening and closing her left elbow after work. She denies any swelling on the tip of her elbow, and notes that it is her non-dominant hand. She reports some weakness, but no numbness or tingling in her hand. She denies any fevers or chills.

The patient states that she has been doing well with her depression. She is currently in therapy and has not been on medication. She denies any suicidal or homicidal ideation.

The patient reports that her last seizure was about a year ago. She has been taking keppra 500 mg twice a day.

The patient reports that she had a peptic ulcer disease endoscopy about a year ago, which showed gastritis. She has been taking protonix 40 mg once a day before meals.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports left elbow pain.
Neurological: Reports left elbow pain.
Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain, shortness of breath.
Gastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain, nausea or vomiting. Endorses nasal congestion.
Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Neurological: Examination of the left elbow reveals erythema and edema. Pain with palpation of the olecranon. Warm to palpation. Pain with flexion and extension of the left elbow. Palpable left radial pulse.

RESULTS

X-ray of the left arm: No evidence of an elbow fracture.

Endoscopy: Gastritis.

ASSESSMENT

1. Left elbow pain.
2. Epilepsy.
3. Depression.
4. Peptic ulcer disease.

PLAN

Ms. Anna is a 26-year-old female with a past medical history significant for epilepsy, depression, and peptic ulcer disease who presents with left elbow pain. She reports pain with flexion and extension of the left elbow. Examination of the left elbow reveals erythema and edema. Pain with palpation of the olecranon. Warm to palpation. Pain with flexion and extension of the left elbow. Palpable left radial pulse. X-ray of the left arm reveals no evidence of an elbow fracture.

For her left elbow pain, I have diagnosed olecranon bursitis. She will receive a corticosteroid injection of meloxicam 15 mg once a day. She will also ice the area. I will provide a left elbow pillow. She will continue to follow up in 2 weeks.

For her epilepsy, she will continue to take keppra 500 mg twice a day. She will receive a refill of keppra 500 mg twice daily.

For her depression, she will start lexapro 10 mg once a day. She will follow up in 2 weeks.

For her peptic ulcer disease, she will continue to take protonix 40 mg once a day before meals.

INSTRUCTIONS

She will follow up in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>
