summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Right knee pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Tyler Nelson is a 72-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for follow-up of his right knee pain. He was last seen in the clinic 2 months ago, where he received a right knee steroid injection. He states that the injection helped his knee pain for about 2 weeks. The patient reports that his knee is still swollen, and the pain level is about a 6 out of 10. He states that he has been wearing compression socks, but it has not been helping with his swelling. He reports that he has slowed down his activities of daily living, and he has to use a cane when getting up from sitting.

The patient reports that he is taking his Xeljanz 11 mg daily, and he has also been taking Celebrex 200 mg daily. He states that he has been taking the medications daily.

The patient reports that his pain is worse when he sits for a while and then has to get up. He states that it is stiff getting up, and he has to use a cane to get his bearings. He reports that after a little bit, it loosens up.

The patient denies any redness or warmth in his leg. He reports that it is just sore like an old bruise.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Examination of the right knee reveals diminished range of motion. There is pitting edema around the knee and ankle. Capillary refill is normal in the toes. The knee is hot to touch.

RESULTS

X-rays of the right knee were ordered.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Right knee pain.
Right knee effusion.

The patient had a right knee fluid aspiration and steroid injection procedure performed today. The risks, benefits, and alternatives were explained. The risks of steroid injection were explained, including, but not limited to: hypopigmentation, soft tissue atrophy, tendon ruptures, and transient hypoglycemia among others. Verbal consent was attained.

The right knee was accessed through the lateral approach with 3 mL of initially clear fluid, then blood-tinged fluid. The right knee was prepped in a standard fashion. The right knee was injected with 80 mg of triamcinolone and 1 mL of lidocaine. The procedure was tolerated well, with no complications.

The patient will follow up with his PCP. He was advised to continue his Xeljanz and Celebrex. He was advised to reduce his salt intake. He was advised to elevate his legs when he sits. He was advised to try compression socks.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>
