summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Pain in belly.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Mr. Bobby is a pleasant 65-year-old male with a history of type 2 diabetes who presents to clinic today for evaluation of severe right upper quadrant pain. The patient reports that he has been experiencing this pain for several weeks. The pain is located on the right hand side of his belly, right underneath his ribs, and is described as a squeezing severe pain. He states that the pain comes and goes, but when it comes, it is really severe and he gets nauseated and sick to his stomach. The pain is rated 8 out of 10. The pain is also associated with eating, and it can come on either immediately after eating or a couple of hours after eating. He has cut out some of the fatty foods that he likes, but he still gets the pain.

The patient states that he has a history of diabetes. He was diagnosed with this about 2 years ago. He has been handling it with diet and exercise. He has lost about 25 pounds over the past 3 months.

The patient denies any fevers. He states that he has had some low-grade fevers and he has felt nauseated, but he has not vomited.

The patient reports that he has a family history of gallbladder disease. His mom and dad both had their gallbladder removed.

The patient states that this pain has not affected his ability to exercise. He likes to get out and take nature photographs. He likes to get out and take pictures of the woods. He has not been to the new park behind the Rex Center. He likes to get down near the river, especially around spring time.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Examination of the heart: Regular rate and rhythm. No ectopic beats, clicks, or murmurs noted.

Examination of the lungs: Clear and equal bilaterally to auscultation.

Examination of the abdomen: Soft, non-distended abdomen. Positive bowel sounds. Positive guarding to palpation of the right upper quadrant. Pain with palpation of the right upper quadrant. No rebound tenderness.

RESULTS

Abdominal ultrasound shows multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. Mild thickening of the gallbladder. Common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

1. Mild cholecystitis with gallstones.
2. Gallbladder inflammation, likely over the last few weeks.

The patient's symptoms have not improved with dietary modifications. I discussed the risks and benefits of cholecystectomy with the patient and recommended a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. He understands the risks and benefits of this procedure.

I recommended that he avoid high fat foods after surgery. I also recommended that he eat foods that are high in fiber. He will follow up with me in 1 week. He will also follow up with his endocrinologist to monitor his diabetes. }}<|eot_id|>
