summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Right knee pain.

SOCIAL HISTORY

Patient reports that he works from home.

MEDICAL HISTORY

Patient reports that he broke his foot three times over the last 10 years. He notes that he has numbness in his foot, but this is not new.

SURGICAL HISTORY

Patient reports that he has had three surgeries for his foot.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain.

VITALS

ECG: Normal.
RESULTS

X-ray of the right knee: No acute fracture or bony abnormality.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Musculoskeletal: Examination of the right knee shows slight edema. There is no pain to palpation of the right lateral knee. Examination of the right knee in all positions is normal. The patient has a positive varus test. There is full range of motion. There is a negative Lachman's sign. The patient has a palpable dorsalis pedis and posterior tibialis pulse.

ASSESSMENT

Right lateral collateral ligament strain.

PLAN

Patient will be referred to physical therapy. The patient will take meloxicam 15 mg once a day. If the patient's symptoms do not improve, we will consider an MRI.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will ice his right knee 20 minutes on and 20 minutes off at least 4 times a day. He will take ibuprofen as needed. If his symptoms get worse, he will call the office. }}<|eot_id|>
