summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Right heel pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Dennis Brown is a 45-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of right heel pain.

The patient is a triathlete who trains 5 days a week. He reports pain in the back of his right heel that worsens after running and when he first wakes up in the morning. The pain is located from the back of his heel to his calf. He denies pain in his left leg. The patient states that the pain is alleviated with activity, but returns after prolonged periods of sitting.

The patient is currently doing 10 km runs, 45-60 minutes of bike rides and 30-60 minutes of swimming, 5 days a week. He does not stretch. He states that he has been doing this for 6 months.

The patient denies any pain when he is walking.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Examination of the lower extremities: The patient reports pain with palpation of the right Achilles tendon. The patient reports pain with squeezing of the right calf. There is no pain with squeezing of the left calf. The Thompson test is negative.

RESULTS

X-rays of the right heel were reviewed and are normal.

ASSESSMENT

Achilles tendinitis.

PLAN

The patient will follow up in 7-10 days after his first physical therapy appointment. I advised the patient to stop training for 1-2 weeks. I recommended Ibuprofen 3 tablets every 6 hours as needed. I advised the patient to call the office for any change in his symptoms.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>
