summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Nonhealing right foot ulcer.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Christina Smith is a pleasant 45-year-old female with a nonhealing right foot ulcer. She reports that she has had this ulcer for approximately one year. She denies any trauma to the foot, and states that it started as a small blister. She states that she had some pain and redness, and was placed on keflex, which resolved. She denies any fever, chills, or drainage from the ulcer. She reports some yellow discharge. She states that she has no pain in the foot, but denies any sensation in the foot. She states that she is feeling tired. She reports that she will be walking around the zoo this weekend. She denies any pain on palpation of the foot.

MEDICAL HISTORY

She reports a past medical history significant for type 2 diabetes.

SOCIAL HISTORY

She reports that she will be walking around the zoo this weekend.

VITALS

All vital signs are within normal limits.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Examination of the right foot reveals a 2 cm x 1 cm circular wound on the dorsal aspect of the lateral right foot, proximal to the 5th metatarsal head. There is yellow slough present with minimal granulation tissue. There is no surrounding erythema or cellulitis. There is no evidence of fluid collection or necrosis. There is no odor and no bony exposure. There is no pain to palpation.

RESULTS

X-ray of the right foot taken today was negative for osteomyelitis.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

1. Diabetic foot ulcer.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient presents with a nonhealing right foot ulcer that has been present for approximately one year. She reports no trauma to the foot and no pain. She reports some yellow discharge from the ulcer. She denies any fever, chills, or drainage from the ulcer. She reports that she has no pain in the foot, but denies any sensation in the foot. She reports that she is feeling tired. She reports that she will be walking around the zoo this weekend. She denies any pain on palpation of the foot. X-ray of the right foot taken today was negative for osteomyelitis. She will have an ABI today.
• Medical Treatment: I will order an ABI today to determine blood supply to the right foot. If the ABI is low, we will consider other options for the foot. I will perform a debridement today. I will prescribe colaganase ointment to apply once daily to the wound, covered with a sterile dressing. We will continue this until we see the wound start to shrink. I will get her a surgical shoe and she will wear that. I will see her back in 2 weeks and will do another debridement if necessary. }}<|eot_id|>
