summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Patient reports concerns regarding medications and heart disease.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Mr. Jose is a 68-year-old male who presents to the clinic for follow-up of his coronary artery disease. He reports that he is doing okay and is concerned about the amount of medications he is taking. The patient had a heart attack approximately 6 months ago and underwent placement of a stent in the left anterior descending artery. He is currently on aspirin, Brilinta, and fish oil. He is compliant with these medications.

The patient reports muscle aches with the use of Crestor. He also reports that he gets dizzy when he stands up quickly. The patient is on Metoprolol 50 mg once a day.

The patient is also concerned about the stents in his heart. He asks how long they will stay open. I explained to him that the stents are permanent and that he will need to continue to take his medications to keep them open. He was advised to continue to follow up with me as well as his primary care physician.

The patient also reports that he has been seeing a dietician, but he is having difficulty with his diet. He reports that his A1c is close to 60. His last blood glucose was 130. He was advised to follow up with his primary care physician regarding his diabetes.

The patient reports that he had a blood test done, which showed that his kidney function is stable. He reports that he has not seen his nephrologist in a year. He was advised to continue to follow up with his nephrologist. He was also advised to avoid Motrin and Advil.

The patient is a fan of the San Francisco 49ers and the LA Rams. He is currently taking Tylenol for headaches.

MEDICATIONS

Patient is currently taking Metoprolol 50 mg once a day, Crestor 40 mg once a day, aspirin, and fish oil. He is compliant with these medications.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Examination of the neck: No jugular venous distension or carotid bruits.
Examination of the heart: S3/6 systolic ejection murmur at the left base.
Examination of the lower extremities: 1+ non-pitting edema.

RESULTS

Kidney function is stable.

ASSESSMENT

1. Coronary artery disease.
2. Stage 3 chronic kidney disease.
3. Diabetes.

PLAN

I discussed the patient's concerns regarding his medications and his heart disease. He is currently on a lot of medications. I advised him to continue to follow up with me and his primary care physician. I referred him to cardiac rehab and advised him to continue to exercise. I also advised him to continue to take his medications.

Regarding his kidney disease, I advised him to follow up with his nephrologist. I also advised him to avoid Motrin and Advil.

The patient is currently having difficulty with his diet and his A1c is high. I advised him to follow up with his primary care physician regarding his diabetes. I advised him to continue to follow up with his primary care physician regarding his insulin regimen.

The patient is currently taking Tylenol for headaches. I advised him to continue to take this medication.

I referred him to psychiatry for depression.

INSTRUCTIONS

Patient will continue to follow up with me and his primary care physician. He will continue to exercise. He will continue to take his medications. He will follow up with his nephrologist. He will avoid Motrin and Advil. He will follow up with his primary care physician regarding his diabetes. He will continue to take Tylenol for headaches. He will follow up with his primary care physician regarding his insulin regimen. }}<|eot_id|>
