summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Abdominal pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Barbara is a 31-year-old female who presents today with abdominal pain.

The patient reports a 1-week history of abdominal pain. She states that she has a longstanding history of diabetes and has been drinking a lot of coffee lately. She believes that this may have triggered her gastritis. She states that the pain is gradually increasing in severity. She reports nausea and vomiting 2-3 times over the past week. She denies fever.

The patient notes that she has been exercising and her abdominal pain spikes when she runs. She denies any shortness of breath.

The patient notes that she has had no asthma attacks recently, but she forgot to take her Flovent 2 times a day. She states that she has been doing well and does not think she needs to continue taking the medication.

The patient notes that her diabetes has been under control. She is currently taking metformin 500 mg once a day. Her blood sugars have been around 800.

The patient denies any recent alcohol use.

MEDICATIONS

• Flovent 44 mcg twice a day
• Albuterol as needed for wheezing
• Metformin 1000 mg in the morning and 500 mg in the evening

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Constitutional: Reports feeling nauseous. Denies fevers.
• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.
• Gastrointestinal: Reports abdominal pain, nausea and vomiting.

PHYSICAL EXAM

• Cardiovascular: Grade 3/6 systolic ejection murmur. Otherwise normal.
• Respiratory: Mild end-expiratory wheezes. Otherwise normal.
• Gastrointestinal: Mild tenderness to palpation of the epigastrium. Otherwise soft, no rebound or guarding.

RESULTS

• Urinalysis: To be sent.
• Urine pregnancy test: To be sent.
• Comprehensive metabolic panel: To be sent.
• CBC: To be sent.

ASSESSMENT

1. Abdominal pain.
2. Diabetes type 2.
3. Asthma.

PLAN

For the first problem of the abdominal pain, I believe that the patient has acute gastritis. I think that this is probably related to the caffeine intake and the moderate history of alcohol use. I want her to cut back on the alcohol intake to less than 2-3 drinks per week. I also want her to cut back on the caffeine. I am going to prescribe Zantac 150 mg twice a day. I will check a urinalysis, urine pregnancy test, CBC, and comprehensive metabolic panel. She will follow up with me in 4 weeks.

For the second problem of diabetes type 2, I want to continue her on the metformin. I am going to increase the morning dose to 1000 mg and the evening dose to 500 mg. She will continue to check her blood sugars and let me know what they are when she comes back. She will follow up with me in 4 weeks.

For the third problem of asthma, she will continue on the Flovent 44 mcg twice a day. She will also continue to take albuterol as needed. }}<|eot_id|>
