summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Right hand pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Alexis Smith is a 65-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of right hand pain.

The patient reports that she works on a computer all day and has been having some numbness and tingling in her right hand. This has been going on for approximately 6 months, but has been more persistent over the last month. The numbness and tingling is present all the time. She denies pain. She states that the pain is worse when she is typing on the keyboard. She has been taking ibuprofen. She denies that it wakes her up at night.

She denies any numbness or tingling in her left hand. She states that she is using her right hand to perform all of her daily activities. She denies any difficulty with her gait or balance.

The patient reports that she is right-handed.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports right hand pain. Denies left hand pain.
Neurological: Reports numbness and tingling in the right hand.
Skin: Denies skin changes.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Neurological: Right hand exam: Grip strength is 5/5. Sensation is decreased in the thumb, index, and middle fingers. Pain to light touch and palpation of the wrist. Positive Tinel's sign.

RESULTS

The patient underwent a right carpal tunnel compression test.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Carpal tunnel syndrome.

Medical Reasoning

The patient presents with right hand pain, numbness, and tingling. The patient reports that she works on a computer all day and has been having some numbness and tingling in her right hand for approximately 6 months, but it has been more persistent over the last month. The numbness and tingling is present all the time. She denies pain. She states that the pain is worse when she is typing on the keyboard. She has been taking ibuprofen. She denies that it wakes her up at night.

The patient reports that she is right-handed.

The patient's symptoms are consistent with carpal tunnel syndrome. The positive Tinel's sign is also consistent with this diagnosis.

Patient Education and Counseling

I discussed the diagnosis and treatment options with the patient today. She understands that I would like to place her in a wrist splint. She will continue to take ibuprofen 600 mg 4 times a day. I also ordered a nerve conduction study of the right hand. If the results are positive, she will be referred for physical therapy. If the results are negative, we will continue with conservative treatment.

Patient Agreements

The patient understands and agrees with the recommended treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will wear the wrist splint at night. She will continue to take ibuprofen 600 mg 4 times a day. }}<|eot_id|>
