summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Acute on chronic CHF exacerbation.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Mr. Johnson is an 82-year-old male with a past medical history significant for hypertension and stage 3 chronic kidney disease who presents for hospital follow-up after an acute on chronic CHF exacerbation.

The patient reports that he was doing well, but noticed that his ankles were swollen and he felt short of breath. He states that he had been trying to watch his diet, but he did slip up and had some pizza and chicken wings. He states that he has been having some dietary indiscretions, but has been trying to watch his diet. He was taken to the ER where his blood pressure was noted to be 200/90. He reports that he has been taking his medications, but sometimes misses a dose or two.

The patient states that he has been watching his diet. He reports that he loves to watch football and go to parties on the weekends and he loves pizza and chicken wings. He states that he cannot help but eat these foods. He reports that he has been having some weight gain and his ankles were swollen.

The patient states that he is doing well now and has been taking his medications. He reports that he is watching his diet. He has been checking his blood pressure at home and it has been good. He denies shortness of breath or problems sleeping. He reports that he has been having a good afternoon nap. He denies chest pain.

The patient reports that he has been following his diet. He reports that he has been watching his diet and taking his medications. He reports that he has been doing well and has not been having any shortness of breath or chest pain.

MEDICATIONS

• Bumex 2 mg PO daily
• Cozaar 100 mg PO daily
• Norvasc 5 mg PO daily

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Constitutional: Denies fevers, chills, or weight loss.
• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain.
• Respiratory: Denies dyspnea on exertion.
• Gastrointestinal: Denies dyspepsia.
• Neurological: Denies headaches.

PHYSICAL EXAM

• Neck: No jugular venous distention.
• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.
• Cardiovascular: Grade 2/6 systolic ejection murmur, which is stable.
• Musculoskeletal: 1+ edema in the lower extremities.

RESULTS

Echocardiogram: Preserved EF of 55%. Abnormal diastolic filling. Mild to moderate mitral regurgitation.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

1. Congestive heart failure.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient's acute on chronic CHF exacerbation was likely caused by dietary indiscretion in uncontrolled hypertension.
• Medical Treatment: Continue Bumex 2 mg PO daily. Continue to watch diet and avoid salty foods. He will be referred to a nutritionist for education on what foods he can eat.
• Patient Education and Counseling: He will weigh himself daily and call me if he gains 3 lbs in 2 days. He will continue to follow his diet and take his medications as prescribed. He will follow up in 3 months.
2. Hypertension.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient has stage 3 chronic kidney disease and uncontrolled hypertension.
• Medical Treatment: Continue Cozaar 100 mg PO daily. Continue Norvasc 5 mg PO daily. Order renal artery ultrasound.
• Patient Education and Counseling: He will follow up in 3 months.
3. Kidney disease.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient has stage 3 chronic kidney disease.
• Medical Treatment: Continue to follow up with labs to ensure he is tolerating his regimen.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up in 3 months. }}<|eot_id|>
