summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Bilateral knee pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Sophia is a pleasant 35-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of bilateral knee pain. The pain has been present for approximately 6 months. She reports that she is unable to recall any specific event that caused the pain. The pain is deep and achy and is located behind her kneecaps. She describes the pain as a deep achy pain. She reports that she can sometimes hear what seems like creaking in her knees. The pain worsens with activities such as getting up from a seated position, watching TV, and going up and down stairs. The pain improves with rest. The patient has tried ice, ibuprofen, and Aleve. She tries to avoid taking any medications.

The patient is an active person who enjoys running. She reports that she used to run 5-6 miles a day but has decreased her mileage to half a mile due to the pain. She notes that she runs outside and on the treadmill. She has not noticed any swelling or redness in her knees. The patient denies any history of knee injuries. She has broken her arm in the past.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports bilateral knee pain.
Neurological: Reports no numbness or tingling in her legs.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Constitutional: In no acute distress.

Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs.

Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.

Musculoskeletal: No overlying erythema, ecchymosis, or warmth of the skin appreciated. There is no effusion. Tenderness to palpation of the bilateral retinacular retinaculum. Positive patellar grind test. Strength is 3/5 for abduction of legs bilaterally. The remainder of muscle strength for the lower extremities is 5/5. Gait is normal. Lachman anterior and posterior drawer and McMurray tests are negative bilaterally.

NEUROLOGIC: Patella and Achilles reflexes are symmetrical.

RESULTS

X-ray of the bilateral knees: No fractures or osteoarthritis.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Bilateral knee pain.
Patellofemoral pain syndrome.

Patient understanding and education: I reviewed the results of her X-ray and explained the diagnosis of patellofemoral pain syndrome. I recommended that she continue to take ibuprofen or anti-inflammatory as needed for pain. I recommended that she start physical therapy to increase her lower extremity strength, mobility, and correct any incorrect running mechanics that she may have. She understands and agrees with the recommended treatment plan.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

She will follow up with me in 2 weeks for a repeat physical exam and to assess her progress. She will also start physical therapy. }}<|eot_id|>
