summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Back pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Kyle Jackson is a 34-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of low back pain. The patient states that he has been having low back pain for the past 2 weeks. He states that it started when he was lifting a box of books and felt a pop. The pain is achy and sharp in nature. He notes that it is radiating down his left leg. The patient states that it did not radiate at first but now it is starting to. He denies any numbness, tingling, or loss of sensation. He denies any loss of strength. He denies any loss of bladder or bowel control. He states that the pain has been getting worse. He has tried taking ibuprofen and Tylenol which sometimes helps. He has not had anyone discuss surgery with him. The patient states that he is an Olympic weightlifter. He notes that he is not as active as he used to be. He denies any family history of spine conditions. He has a history of playing soccer. He denies smoking.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports low back pain.
Neurological: Denies numbness, tingling, or loss of sensation. Denies loss of strength.

PHYSICAL EXAM

CV: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, rubs, or gallops.
Respiratory: Clear to auscultation bilaterally.
MSK: No overlying redness or bruising of the skin. Midline tenderness at the L4-L5 disk space with right-sided lumbar paravertebral tenderness. Pain with lumbar flexion. Pain with lumbar extension. Supine straight leg test is positive.
NEURO: Decreased sensation to light touch of the left lower extremity. Patella and Achilles reflexes are symmetrical.

RESULTS

Mri of the lumbar spine shows a disk desiccation, disk bulge with paracentral disk herniation resulting in moderate right neuroforaminal stenosis.

ASSESSMENT

Lumbar back pain at the L4-L5 disk space due to a herniated disk with radiculopathy.

PLAN

I reviewed the patient’s results and discussed them with him. I explained to the patient that the MRI of his lumbar spine shows a disk desiccation, disk bulge with paracentral disk herniation resulting in moderate right neuroforaminal stenosis. He is experiencing symptoms consistent with a herniated disk with nerve impingement. I recommended a spinal injection with a strong NSAID and corticosteroid. This will help with the inflammation that is causing his pain. I explained to him that this is a typical procedure that I do all the time and it is in-office. He is a good candidate for this procedure today. I discussed the risks associated with the procedure with him. He has agreed to proceed with the procedure. }}<|eot_id|>
