summary is: {{ Patient: Christopher Watson, 86-year-old male.
Date of birth: 04/12/1934.
Reason for presentation: Follow-up for adult hydrocephalus.

Medical history: 8-10 years of progressive gait impairment, cognitive impairment, and decreased bladder control.
Diagnosis of adult hydrocephalus with a spino catheter protocol in June 2020, and underwent shunt surgery on August 1st, 2020. Medtronic Strata programmable shunt in the ventricular peritoneal configuration was placed.

PMHx: Not significant.

DHx: Not significant.

SHx: Has been doing better since shunt surgery.

Impression: Adult hydrocephalus, gait impairment, urinary incontinence and urgency, and cognitive impairment.

Plan: To leave the shunt set for another 2-3 months before considering changing it. To continue to monitor for any changes in symptoms. To continue to wear a diaper for urinary incontinence. To consider enrolling in a driver safety program.

Impression: Adult hydrocephalus, gait impairment, urinary incontinence and urgency, and cognitive impairment.

Plan: To leave the shunt set for another 2-3 months before considering changing it. To continue to monitor for any changes in symptoms. To continue to wear a diaper for urinary incontinence. To consider enrolling in a driver safety program. }}<|eot_id|>
