summary is: {{ History of present illness: 
Patient presents with a 3-4 week history of right upper quadrant (RUQ) pain which is exacerbated by eating fatty foods. Pain is described as severe and nauseating, rated as 8/10. He has also had some fevers which are low grade and has not vomited. He has had some improvement in his symptoms with diet modification but the pain persists. 

Past medical history: 
Patient is a type 2 diabetic and has a family history of gallstones in both parents who had their gallbladder removed.

Social history: 
Patient is a photographer and enjoys taking nature pictures. He has not had any recent changes in his work or social activities.

Physical examination: 
Vital signs: BP 128/88, pulse 68, RR 16, oxygen saturation 99%. He is afebrile.

Abdominal examination: 
He has a soft and non-distended abdomen. There is guarding in the RUQ. He is tender in the RUQ but there is no rebound tenderness. 

Impression: 
Impression of cholecystitis with gallstones. 

Plan: 
Laparoscopic cholecystectomy. He will need to avoid fatty foods and high fibre diet. He will need to follow up in 1 week after the surgery. He will need to follow up with his primary care physician for his diabetes. }}<|eot_id|>
