summary is: {{ History of Present Illness: 
Patient, 67-year-old male, presents with a 4-day history of right shoulder pain. He reports that he had an injury to his right shoulder while playing football. He has been having difficulty lifting and moving his arm since the injury. He has tried taking Tylenol for the pain but it has not provided significant relief. He reports that his symptoms are unchanged since the injury. 
Past Medical History: 
Patient has had an appendectomy 3-4 years ago. He has no other significant medical history. 
Social History: 
Patient is a football player. 
Physical Examination: 
Patient is able to lift his arm to shoulder height but has pain in the posterior aspect of the shoulder. He has limited abduction and adduction of the arm to 90 degrees. He has pain with supination and pronation of the arm. He has a palpable radial pulse. 
Imaging: 
X-ray of the right shoulder shows an acute acromioclavicular joint fracture. 
Impression: 
Acute acromioclavicular joint fracture. 
Plan: 
Prescribe meloxicam 15mg PO daily for pain control. 
Order a CT scan of the right shoulder to further evaluate the extent of the fracture. 
Refer to physical therapy for shoulder strengthening exercises. 
Avoid contact sports until the fracture heals. 
Advise patient to avoid heavy lifting and to use a sling to support the shoulder. }}<|eot_id|>
