summary is: {{ HPC: 25-year-old male presents with a 4-month history of lower back pain, which is worse when sitting and bending. He had initially hurt his back while moving boxes, and has been having difficulty supporting his upper body weight and numbness in his toes, which has since resolved. He has had a persistent sharp pain in his lower back, worse when lying down and rolling over. He has tried heat, ice, tylenol, and ibuprofen, with no relief. He has had no incontinence or difficulty with bowel movements. He has had no fever or chills. He has no significant medical history other than a total knee replacement 2 years ago. He has no PMHx of any other medical problems. 

Impression: Acute disk herniation, lumbar spine. 

Plan: 

1. Referral to physical therapy for lumbar spine exercises to strengthen the muscles and improve posture.
2. Meloxicam 15mg once a day to reduce inflammation and pain.
3. Follow up in 2 weeks to assess progress and consider further treatment options if necessary. }}<|eot_id|>
