summary is: {{ History of present illness: The patient, a 56-year-old female, presents with a 4-day history of right elbow pain. She has been experiencing pain on the medial aspect of her elbow, which worsens with activities such as pottery and baking. She has not been able to work on her pottery recently due to the pain, which is rated as a 7/10. She has been using ibuprofen for pain relief but has not noticed any improvement. She has been having difficulty sleeping at night due to the pain.

Past medical history: Hypertension, type 2 diabetes mellitus, and dyslipidaemia.

Medications: Lisinopril 10mg daily, metformin 500mg twice daily.

Social history: She enjoys pottery and baking, and is currently making a ceramic bowl for her son's birthday. She has been having trouble keeping up with her diabetes management and has been having difficulty controlling her blood sugars.

Physical examination: Tenderness on the medial epicondyle of the right elbow. Pain with resisted pronation of the right forearm. No signs of swelling or bruising.

Impression: Medial epicondylitis.

Plan: Rest the right arm in a sling for the next few weeks. Apply ice to the elbow for 20 minutes three times a day. Take 600mg of ibuprofen three times a day with food. Continue metformin at the same dose. Increase metformin to 1000mg twice daily. Follow up in 2 weeks. Consider referral to a physical therapist for further evaluation and management. }}<|eot_id|>
