summary is: {{ History of Present Illness: 
The patient is a 58-year-old female who was recently admitted to the hospital for an interior ST elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI) and had another stent placed. She had a history of coronary artery disease and hypertension. She is on medication for her hypertension and is taking metformin for her diabetes. She is able to walk her dog daily, but is not able to exercise vigorously. She has been having some chest pain and shortness of breath, but is not experiencing any chest pain or shortness of breath at the moment.

Past Medical History: 
The patient has a history of coronary artery disease, hypertension, and diabetes. She has had a stent placed in the past and has been taking medication for her hypertension and diabetes. She has a history of smoking and has been a heavy smoker in the past.

Social History: 
The patient is married and has a dog. She is able to walk her dog daily and enjoys walking. She likes to eat pizza and is able to cook for herself.

Family History: 
The patient has a family history of hypertension and heart disease.

Physical Examination: 
The patient is in no acute distress. Her blood pressure is 130/80 mmHg, pulse is 72 beats per minute, and oxygen saturation is 98% on room air. Her lungs are clear to auscultation and percussion. Her heart sounds are normal with no murmurs or rubs. Her abdomen is soft and nontender. Her extremities are normal with no edema. Her radial pulses are 2+ on the right and 1+ on the left. Her distal pulses are 2+ on the right and 1+ on the left. Her lower extremities are normal with no edema or tenderness.

